# Built from the essay studies/data/notes/septuagint-myth-notes.html, in its order and under its own headings.
# The verses the essay quotes (Matthew 5:18, Isaiah 7:14) are pulled from the KJV by the builder.

=== Introduction
--- The Septuagint Myth [title]
overline: Baptist & church history
heading: The Septuagint Myth
sub: Debunking the 2nd Century BC Narrative
refs: Matthew 5:18; Isaiah 7:14
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

--- A historical fraud
lead: The traditional narrative of the Septuagint (LXX), a supposed 2nd-century BC Greek translation of the Old Testament (OT), is a historical fraud that cannot withstand scrutiny.
p: The claim—rooted in the pseudepigraphic Letter of Aristeas—that 72 Jewish translators from all 12 tribes, commissioned by Ptolemy II, produced a unified Greek OT is not only implausible but contradicted by manuscript evidence, textual analysis, and biblical context.

--- God’s preserved word
p: As a defender of God’s preserved word in the Hebrew Masoretic Text (MT) and Byzantine Majority Text, I argue that the LXX, as known today in 4th-century AD manuscripts like Codex Vaticanus and Codex Sinaiticus, is a Roman Catholic fabrication, revised to align with New Testament (NT) quotations and shaped by the apostate Catholic Church’s agenda to alter scripture.
callout: Below, I synthesize my case, grounded in the King James Bible (KJV), exposing the LXX myth and its unreliable minority text foundations.

=== The Letter of Aristeas: A Forgery
--- The Letter of Aristeas
lead: The Letter of Aristeas, dated to the 2nd century BC, claims Ptolemy II (285–246 BC) commissioned 72 translators—six from each of Israel’s 12 tribes—to translate the Torah into Greek for the Library of Alexandria.
callout: This story is a fabrication.
p: No Egyptian records corroborate Ptolemy’s commission, and the letter’s miraculous account of identical translations reeks of legend, not history.

--- Anachronistic nonsense
panel-warn: The 10 northern tribes | The involvement of scribes from the 10 northern tribes, dispersed by Assyria in 722 BC ([[2 Kings 17:6]]), is anachronistic nonsense, as these tribes were effectively lost.
panel-warn: The Levites | Even non-Levite scribes translating sacred texts stretches credulity, given the Levites’ exclusive role in preserving scripture ([[Deuteronomy 31:25-26]]).
callout: The Letter is a pseudepigraphic attempt to legitimize Greek texts for Hellenistic Jews, not a historical record.

=== Scant Manuscript Evidence
--- Scraps, not a Septuagint
lead: Manuscript evidence demolishes the idea of a 2nd-century BC Septuagint.
p: Only 1.7–2.6% of the OT survives in Greek from BC sources, per fragments like 4Q120 (Leviticus, 1st century BC) and Papyrus Fouad 266 (Deuteronomy, 2nd–1st century BC). These scraps, covering parts of the Torah and Minor Prophets, are far from a complete OT.

--- Over 500 years later
step: 2nd–1st c. BC | Papyrus Fouad 266 (Deuteronomy).
step: 1st c. BC | 4Q120 (Leviticus).
step: 1st c. AD | No unified Greek OT manuscript exists from the 1st century AD, when the NT was written.
step: 4th c. AD | The earliest complete LXX texts—Codex Vaticanus and Codex Sinaiticus—date to the 4th century AD, over 500 years after the supposed translation.

--- The MT and the Dead Sea Scrolls
p: These minority texts, riddled with thousands of variants (e.g., 6,000–7,000 NT differences), lack the reliability of the harmonious MT, which aligns closely with the Dead Sea Scrolls (DSS, 250 BC–70 AD).
callout: The DSS, with over 200 Hebrew manuscripts, confirm the MT’s stability, while Greek fragments show textual diversity, suggesting ad hoc translations, not a standardized Septuagint.

=== Hebrew Primacy in the NT Era
--- One jot or one tittle
lead: The NT assumes a Hebrew textual context, not a Greek one. In [[Matthew 5:18]] (KJV), Jesus declares:
big: Matthew 5:18 | mark: Till heaven and earth pass, one jot or one tittle shall in no wise pass from the law
p: “Jot” (Hebrew yod, smallest letter) and “tittle” (scribal stroke) refer to Hebrew script, meaningless in Greek.

--- Hebrew scrolls in the synagogues
p: This underscores that Hebrew scrolls, not a Greek LXX, were read in 1st-century Judean synagogues ([[Luke 4:16-20]]). The DSS, overwhelmingly Hebrew, and rabbinic practices (e.g., Mishnah, Megillah 4:4) confirm Hebrew’s primacy.
p: While Greek was spoken, NT writers like Paul, fluent in Hebrew, likely translated Hebrew texts into Greek for their audience, not quoted a non-existent Septuagint.
callout: Similarities between NT quotations (e.g., [[Isaiah 7:14]], “virgin” in [[Matthew 1:23]]) and later LXX texts (e.g., parthenos) result from natural translation of Hebrew terms like almah (virgin or young maiden), not reliance on a unified Greek OT.

=== Almah and Translation Integrity
--- A virgin shall conceive
lead: The claim that NT writers quoted an LXX misreading of almah in [[Isaiah 7:14]] is baseless.
big: Isaiah 7:14 | mark: a virgin shall conceive
p: Almah, used seven times in the OT (e.g., [[Genesis 24:43]], Rebekah as virgin), denotes a young, unmarried woman, often implying virginity in context ([[Song of Solomon 6:8]]).

--- Parthenos
p: The NT’s parthenos ([[Matthew 1:23]]) accurately renders almah’s meaning, reflecting the miraculous sign of a virgin birth. Similarities with 4th-century LXX texts are due to shared translation choices, not NT dependence on a 2nd-century BC Septuagint.
p: Like KJV translators, who used English terms (e.g., “charity” for agape) to convey meaning, NT writers rendered Hebrew into Greek, preserving sense over literalism.
callout: This explains overlaps without requiring a mythical LXX.

=== 4th-Century Christian Revision
--- A 4th-century Roman Catholic product
lead: The LXX, as preserved in Vaticanus and Sinaiticus, is a 4th-century AD Roman Catholic product, revised to align with NT quotations.
p: These minority texts, produced in Christian scriptoria, show deliberate harmonization. For example, Isaiah 7:14’s parthenos in Vaticanus matches Matthew 1:23, supporting the virgin birth, while the MT’s almah is ambiguous.

--- Christian scribes editing
step: 240 AD | Origen’s Hexapla
step: 300 AD | The Lucianic recension
step: 382–405 AD | Jerome’s Vulgate
p: Origen’s Hexapla (240 AD) and the Lucianic recension (300 AD) demonstrate Christian scribes editing Greek OT texts, often to reflect NT theology.

--- Catholic tampering
p: The Catholic Church, an apostate institution of the 4th century, sponsored this revisionism, as seen in Jerome’s Vulgate (382–405 AD), which included the Apocrypha despite early fathers like Melito and Jerome questioning their canonicity.
callout: The Apocrypha’s inclusion, absent from the Jewish MT and warned against by Athanasius, reflects Catholic tampering, not divine preservation.

=== Unreliable Minority Texts
--- Vaticanus and Sinaiticus
lead: Vaticanus and Sinaiticus, the backbone of the LXX, are unreliable.
p: Their 4th-century dating, largely affirmed by Catholic-controlled scholarship (e.g., Vatican Library for Vaticanus), faces providential questions. Sinaiticus’ authenticity is further doubted due to 19th-century forgery claims by Constantine Simonides, who alleged creating it in 1839–1840.
p: While paleography supports their antiquity, their 6,000–7,000 NT variants contrast with the Byzantine Majority Text’s harmony, which underpins the KJV.

--- Westcott and Hort
panel-warn: Westcott and Hort | Westcott and Hort, 19th-century heretics with liberal biases, championed these texts to undermine the Textus Receptus, driven by profit and unorthodox agendas.
callout: Their work, like Catholic dating, cannot be trusted against the MT’s fidelity, confirmed by the DSS.

=== Objections Answered
--- Objections answered (1 of 2)
panel-warn: Objection | BC Greek fragments (e.g., 4Q120) prove a 2nd-century BC Septuagint.
panel: Response | These fragments, covering only 1.7–2.6% of the OT, are disparate, not a unified text. They reflect localized translations, not a Ptolemaic commission.
panel-warn: Objection | NT quotations (50–80% LXX-like) suggest a pre-Christian Septuagint.
panel: Response | Similarities stem from NT writers translating Hebrew texts, like almah to parthenos. Vaticanus/Sinaiticus were later harmonized to match NT, not quoted in the 1st century.

--- Objections answered (2 of 2)
panel-warn: Objection | Jewish use (e.g., Philo) and early papyri show an early LXX.
panel: Response | Philo cited individual Greek translations, not a standardized Septuagint. Papyri (e.g., Chester Beatty, 2nd–3rd century AD) are Christian, postdating the NT and reflecting revision, not a 2nd-century BC text.
panel-warn: Objection | Catholic scholarship is ecumenical, not controlling dating.
panel: Response | The Catholic Church’s history of altering scripture (Vulgate, Apocrypha) and control of Vaticanus taint its credibility. Its apostate status by the 4th century undermines its role as a neutral arbiter.

=== Conclusion
--- The 2nd-century BC Septuagint is a myth
lead: The 2nd-century BC Septuagint is a myth, unsupported by manuscripts, contradicted by Hebrew primacy in the NT ([[Matthew 5:18]]), and exposed as a 4th-century AD revision in unreliable minority texts like Vaticanus and Sinaiticus.
p: The Letter of Aristeas is a forgery, BC fragments are negligible, and NT-LXX similarities reflect translation, not a unified Greek OT. The Catholic Church’s apostate influence, from the Vulgate to Apocrypha inclusion, and the dubious scholarship of Westcott and Hort, confirm the LXX’s corruption.

--- Uphold the true scriptures [title]
overline: Baptist & church history
heading: Uphold the true scriptures
sub: God’s preserved word lies in the MT, aligned with the DSS, and the Byzantine Majority Text, faithfully rendered in the KJV. Let us reject the LXX myth and uphold the true scriptures.
